 job to finish." CAL: So... what's all this?"CAL: May I?"CAL: Let's see...",It's still operational. Must be modern."DLooks like it's for sorting and classifying excavated minerals."-It has a data set loaded, but I don't..."ECAL: Yeah, I don't think the minerals are actually here anymore."HAnd naturally it says it's locked until the properties are matched."Give me a sec..."}Computers aren't exactly my thing, but this interface is apparently built so that even empty- headed miners can work it."0You just have to... push the blocks around?"8On second thought, that seems horribly inefficient."èWell, there are some details here about the minerals, and if we want to unlock the way I guess we'll have to go over them and connect the dots."Or something."PI'll summarize this and then you can talk to me if you want to get started.""Complete.""CAL: All right."So the gist is..."=I've got a list of details about these missing minerals."5They'll have to serve as our clues, so to speak."ELooks like there's exactly four of them, and one for each color."ÄIt's pretty incomplete, but we'll have to do our best to fill in the holes and figure out the properties each color mineral had."oOnce we have an idea of it, we'll push the matching blocks into position, matching the screens above them."VThe screens each display properties the minerals can have, or a category thereof."So if we decided that the Emerald had a Hardness of 5, we'd have to push that green block in front of the screen that says "5"."5Uh, a lower Hardness means it's softer, I guess?"o...Frankly, I'm not so sure these are the actual scientific properties, but... whatever they say, I guess."POnce everything's in place, I can check it against the registered data set."4If it's a perfect match, then it should unlock.""So, how do you want to start?"CAL: What's the word?")CAL: All right, let's take a look..."jCAL: I get the feeling pen and paper might be helpful for this, if there's anything like that around."What a me